So, in particular, suppose that the account secret token was like ABC.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:50.000
Well, then if the token that we supply is like D, we look at A, we look at D, we compare them,

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.800
we see that it's false, and we return no. But what if it was a little bit more similar,

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.960
like it was AE? Well, then triple equals would look at both A's.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.920
It would say, okay, looks good. It would move on to B and E.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:03.680
Oh, they're not the same. It would return false.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.120
But if you pay attention, you notice that it takes a little bit longer before it gets

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.480
to returning false in the second case because the prefix, this A and A, were actually the same.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.480
That means that if a malevolent attacker was sitting around with their stopwatch going,

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.800
and they had all the time in the world, they could brute force a bunch of strings to try

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:29.280
and see which strings take a little bit longer than others, at which point it would figure out

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.760
that it was getting closer and closer to the real token that it wouldn't otherwise know,

